    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "You know, it's interesting, though. Is it a tale full of sound and fury signifying nothing? I'll leave out the middle part, because we know who the nominee is going to be, or is it possible we don't know who the nominee is going to? Here's the thing. Donald Trump is polling about, what, 50% and no one is anywhere close. It tells us, naturally, that there's another 50%ish, 40 to 50% in the Republican voters field that doesn't want Trump or is looking at least at some of the other candidates are showing initial support for the other candidates. There's four left standing. This goes on and on to what end. I don't know. It's not and it's not as if each of those four come from the same perspective so that you could have a unified candidate representing that non-Trump 50%. They all kind of have a different angle and read in the conservative movement on the Republican Party. Vivek Ramaswami is not like Ron DeSantis. Nikki Haley is not like any of them. And Chris Christie is kind of just his own thing. So it's not like they could get together or their donors could get together and say, OK, three of you got to go. And we got to satisfy the other than Trump candidacy with one other candidate. That would in its own right make sense. But it can't possibly happen. I'm not sure what you think about any of this. Um, it may all be fruitless anyway. I mean, I think Donald Trump is the, I think he's the nominee.",
