    "turn_text": "Yeah. Well, it raises an interesting question for our politics today. It's a question that the book brought up in my mind, John, because we're going through this. Lori Roberts had a column the other day. We're going through this thing right now. Well, we have a lot of moderate Republicans or a lot of liberal Republicans who can't seem to bring themselves to, well, they're tempted by independence. Sometimes they're tempted by Democrats. They can't bring themselves to vote for a Republican who is too far to the right as they say or just not their flavor of Republican. And it dawned on me that Barry Goldwater's candidacies have taught a couple of lessons when he ran for president. You know, there was a lot of that in 1964. A lot of Republicans denounced him. The Scrantons, the Rockefellers, the Romneys, they denounced him and it carried out through the election for far too many. I was looking up the numbers. Goldwater got seven million fewer votes in 1964 than Richard Nixon did in 1960. exit polling was at best in those days rudimentary so it's hard to know how many republicans actually voted for johnson or didn't vote for goldwater but he ended up getting seven million fewer votes than richard nixon nonetheless and it dawns on me no matter what kind of republican you are certainly you would look at the johnson years nineteen sixty four to nineteen sixty nine as years that have left us with It really almost everything every Republican would argue against, which is an entrenched welfare state, an expanded bureaucracy, an inability really to touch the budget because of growing concerns that come out of these programs you cannot touch because there must fund programs that have been ever growing. The great society to just take one aspect of what the Goldwater loss yielded to the problems every republicans still complains about today and i wonder what would be like to go back to those nineteen sixty four republicans who couldn't bring themselves to vote for gold water and ask how that worked out for them but it's a message for republicans today",
